/* QUANT TEST LAB — REASONING TRACK Learn content.
   Same shape and depth standard as js/lessons.js (see concept 'c3' there for
   the reference bar): every primer explains its vocabulary from zero before
   using it, every concept ships 3 worked examples and 6 progressive checks.
   track:'reasoning' on every unit. */
(function (global) {
  'use strict';

  const L = [

    /* =============================== R-A =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'R-A', topic: 'Numerical Reasoning', title: 'Numerical sequences: a systematic diagnosis', track: 'reasoning', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'ra1', name: 'What a sequence is, and the seven-question checklist for finding its rule',
          primer: 'A "sequence" is simply an ordered list of numbers, written one after another, where the order matters — the list 2, 4, 6, 8 is a different sequence from 8, 6, 4, 2 even though it contains the same four numbers. Each individual number in the list is called a "term," and terms are usually numbered starting from the first one: the "first term" is the leftmost number, the "second term" is the next one, and so on. A sequence "rule" (sometimes called its "pattern") is the hidden, fixed procedure that was used to generate every term from the ones before it — a timed reasoning test never asks you to guess a rule out of thin air; it always gives you enough consecutive terms that the SAME rule, applied consistently, produces every one of them, and your job is to reverse-engineer that rule quickly enough to predict the next term (or fill in a missing one). Because there are only a handful of rule "families" that these tests draw from, the fastest way to work under time pressure is not creative guessing but a fixed CHECKLIST you run through in order, stopping as soon as one test succeeds: (1) Are the terms increasing or decreasing by the same fixed amount every time? Compute "term minus the previous term" for each consecutive pair — this is called the first difference — and see if it is constant; if so, the sequence is "arithmetic." (2) If not, is each term the previous term multiplied by the same fixed number? Compute the ratio of consecutive terms; if constant, the sequence is "geometric." (3) If neither differences nor ratios are constant, take the differences of the differences (the "second difference"); if THAT is constant, the sequence is "quadratic" — its differences themselves grow arithmetically. (4) If the differences look irregular in a rhythmic way, check whether splitting the list into odd-numbered and even-numbered positions reveals two much simpler sequences hiding inside one — this is called "interleaved." (5) Check whether each term is built from the TWO terms before it (typically by adding them) rather than just the one immediately before — this is the Fibonacci-style pattern. (6) Check whether the OPERATION itself seems to alternate (e.g. multiply, then add, then multiply, then add) rather than staying fixed. (7) As a last resort, check whether each term matches a simple function of its position in the list, like (position)² or (position)³ plus a constant. Running this checklist in order, rather than randomly trying ideas, is what makes fast, reliable pattern-spotting possible under a 20-second clock.',
          core: 'Diagnose a numeric sequence with a fixed checklist, in order: constant difference (arithmetic) → constant ratio (geometric) → constant second difference (quadratic) → split into odd/even positions (interleaved) → depends on the two previous terms (Fibonacci-style) → alternating operations → a function of position (power-based). Stop at the first one that fits.',
          when: 'Any "what comes next" or "fill in the missing term" numeric sequence question.',
          formulas: ['First difference: d(n) = term(n+1) − term(n). Constant ⇒ arithmetic, next term = last + d.', 'Ratio: q(n) = term(n+1) / term(n). Constant ⇒ geometric, next term = last × q.', 'Second difference constant ⇒ quadratic: keep extending the difference row by its own constant step, then add that new difference to the last term.', 'Fibonacci-style: term(n) = term(n−1) + term(n−2), or a weighted version term(n) = term(n−1) + m·term(n−2).'],
          intuition: 'Every family above is really the SAME idea applied to a different "layer" of the sequence: arithmetic looks at the raw terms, quadratic looks one layer deeper (differences of differences), and interleaved / Fibonacci-style / alternating-operation all just change WHICH earlier terms a new term depends on. Running the checklist in a fixed order stops you from re-deriving this reasoning from scratch every time.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'What comes next: 5, 9, 13, 17, ?  Step 1 of the checklist: first differences are 9−5=4, 13−9=4, 17−13=4 — constant. The sequence is arithmetic with common difference 4, so the next term is 17+4 = 21.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'What comes next: 3, 6, 12, 24, ?  Step 1: first differences are 3, 6, 12 — not constant, so not arithmetic. Step 2: ratios are 6/3=2, 12/6=2, 24/12=2 — constant. The sequence is geometric with common ratio 2, so the next term is 24×2 = 48.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'What comes next: 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, ?  Step 1: first differences are 3, 5, 7, 9 — not constant, so not arithmetic. Step 2: ratios (5/2=2.5, 10/5=2, ...) are not constant either, so not geometric. Step 3: second differences (differences of 3,5,7,9) are 2, 2, 2 — constant! The sequence is quadratic. The differences continue 9, then 9+2=11, so the next term is 26+11 = 37.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Stopping the checklist too early — e.g. concluding "not arithmetic" after checking only the first two differences instead of all of them, or trying to force an arithmetic answer onto a sequence whose differences are clearly still changing.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'What comes next: 8, 14, 20, 26, ?', options: ['30', '32', '34', '28'], a: 1, why: 'First differences are all 6 (constant) — arithmetic. Next term = 26+6 = 32.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'What is the FIRST test you should run on an unfamiliar numeric sequence?', options: ['Check for a constant ratio', 'Check for a constant first difference', 'Assume it is quadratic', 'Guess and check against the options'], a: 1, why: 'The checklist starts with the simplest structure — a constant first difference (arithmetic) — before trying anything more complex.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'What comes next: 2, 6, 18, 54, ?', options: ['108', '162', '81', '216'], a: 1, why: 'Ratios are all 3 (constant) — geometric. Next term = 54×3 = 162.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A sequence has first differences 4, 4, 4, 4. What KIND of sequence is it?', options: ['Geometric', 'Arithmetic', 'Quadratic', 'Fibonacci-style'], a: 1, why: 'A constant first difference is the defining test for an arithmetic sequence.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'What comes next: 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, ?', options: ['14', '15', '16', '18'], a: 2, why: 'First differences: 1,2,3,4 — not constant. Second differences: 1,1,1 — constant, so quadratic. Next difference is 5, so next term = 11+5 = 16.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'What comes next: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ?', options: ['11', '12', '13', '10'], a: 2, why: 'Neither differences nor ratios are constant, but each term equals the sum of the two terms before it (Fibonacci-style): 5+8 = 13.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'ra2', name: 'Number grids and odd-one-out: finding a rule across two dimensions, or spotting a broken property',
          primer: 'A "number grid" in these tests is almost always a small square table (commonly 3×3, meaning 3 rows and 3 columns) filled with numbers according to one consistent rule, with one cell replaced by a question mark that you must fill in. The key difference from a simple sequence is that a grid rule can depend on TWO separate directions at once: how a cell changes as you move ACROSS a row (left to right), and how it changes as you move DOWN a column (top to bottom) — these are two independent pieces of information, and a grid question is really asking you to find both of them separately, then combine them. The simplest grids only vary in one direction: every row might follow the exact same "add a fixed amount moving right" rule, in which case you only need the missing cell\'s OWN row to solve it, ignoring the other rows entirely. Harder grids combine a row-step and a column-step simultaneously, so that the value in a cell equals some starting number, plus (its row position) times a fixed "row step," plus (its column position) times a fixed "column step" — here you must isolate both steps by comparing several cells before you can solve for the missing one. A completely different task, "odd one out," gives you five or six numbers where five of them all share one specific mathematical property (such as being even, being a multiple of 3, being a perfect square, or being a prime number) and exactly one number breaks that shared property; your job is to correctly name the shared property — not just notice "these numbers feel similar" — and then confirm which single number fails to fit it. The reason naming the property explicitly matters so much is that many number sets share MULTIPLE properties at once by coincidence, and if you lock onto the wrong one (say, "these are all even" when the intended property was actually "these are all multiples of 4"), you can mis-identify the outlier.',
          core: 'For grids: test whether the rule changes only along rows, only along columns, or both at once (value = base + row-step×row + column-step×column), then apply it to the missing cell\'s own position. For odd-one-out: state the shared property in words BEFORE checking each number against it.',
          when: 'A 3×3 (or similar) table of numbers with one cell missing, or a short list of numbers where you must identify the one that does not share a stated or implied property with the rest.',
          formulas: ['Row-only grid: missing value = (another value in the same row) ± (row\'s constant step) × (number of columns apart).', 'Two-direction grid: cell(row, col) = base + row×(row step) + col×(column step), counting row/col from 0.', 'Odd-one-out: state the shared rule R explicitly, then check each candidate against R — the one that fails R is the answer.'],
          intuition: 'A grid is just two overlapping sequences at once — one running across, one running down — so the same "find the constant step" instinct from ordinary sequences still applies, just twice, in two directions. Odd-one-out inverts the usual task: instead of predicting a hidden term, you are naming a hidden RULE first, and only then checking who breaks it.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A grid has rows [3, 7, 11], [9, 13, 17], [2, 6, 10], and the missing cell replaces the "13" in the middle row. Every row increases by 4 moving left to right (7−3=4, 11−7=4; 13−9=4, 17−13=4; 6−2=4, 10−6=4), so this is a row-only rule. The missing cell is one step after 9 in its row: 9+4 = 13.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Which number does not belong: 6, 12, 18, 24, 25? State the shared property first: 6, 12, 18, 24 are all multiples of 6. Checking 25 against that property: 25 ÷ 6 is not a whole number, so 25 is the odd one out.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A grid\'s first row is 2, 5, 8 (moving right adds 3) and its first column is 2, 9, 16 (moving down adds 7). The missing cell is at row 3, column 3 (using 1-indexed counting, so row index 2 and column index 2 from 0). Using the two-direction formula with base 2: cell = 2 + (row index)×7 + (column index)×3 = 2 + 2×7 + 2×3 = 2+14+6 = 22.' }
          ],
          trap: 'In a two-direction grid, testing only one row or only one column and assuming the whole grid follows that single step — always cross-check against a second row or column before trusting a row-only or column-only conclusion. In odd-one-out, latching onto the first shared property you notice (like "all even") when a more specific property (like "all multiples of 4") is the one that actually isolates a unique outlier.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'In a grid where every row increases by 5 moving left to right, what do you need to find a missing cell?', options: ['Every other row in the grid', 'Only the missing cell\'s own row', 'The average of the whole grid', 'The column totals'], a: 1, why: 'A row-only rule means each row is independent — you only need the values already visible in that same row.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Which number does not belong: 4, 8, 12, 16, 15?', options: ['4', '15', '12', '16'], a: 1, why: '4, 8, 12, 16 are all multiples of 4; 15 is not.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Which number does not belong: 9, 16, 25, 36, 40?', options: ['9', '40', '25', '16'], a: 1, why: '9, 16, 25, 36 are all perfect squares (3², 4², 5², 6²); 40 is not.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A grid\'s cells follow cell(row, col) = 1 + row×3 + col×2 (counting from 0). What is cell at row 2, column 1?', options: ['7', '9', '8', '10'], a: 1, why: '1 + 2×3 + 1×2 = 1+6+2 = 9.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Which number does not belong: 2, 3, 5, 9, 7, 11?', options: ['9', '2', '7', '11'], a: 0, why: '2, 3, 5, 7, 11 are all prime numbers; 9 = 3×3 is not.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A grid\'s first row is 4, 9, 14 and first column is 4, 10, 16. Using cell(row,col) = base + row×(row step) + col×(col step) with 0-indexing, what is the value at row 3, column 3 (i.e. row index 2, column index 2)?', options: ['24', '26', '22', '28'], a: 1, why: 'Row step = 6 (10−4), column step = 5 (9−4). Cell = 4 + 2×6 + 2×5 = 4+12+10 = 26.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== R-B =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'R-B', topic: 'Abstract Reasoning', title: 'Pattern reasoning with symbols — and its honest limits', track: 'reasoning', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'rb1', name: 'What this section can train, what it cannot, and how to read symbol cycles and grids',
          primer: 'Real "abstract reasoning" or "spatial reasoning" test sections, as used by many employers, show you actual PICTURES of abstract shapes — arrows, dots, rotated and reflected polygons arranged in a 3×3 matrix — and ask you to pick the image that completes the pattern, often requiring you to mentally rotate or reflect a figure in two or three dimensions. That is a genuinely different mental skill from anything text can fully replicate, and honesty matters here: this app cannot show you real rotating images, so this section instead uses TEXT and simple unicode symbols (▲ ● ■ ◆ ★, and similar) arranged into cycles and grids that follow rules of the same general FLAVOUR as visual matrix reasoning — spotting a repeating structure, tracking how a count or a position changes — but it is not a substitute for practicing with real rotated-figure matrices if your actual assessment uses them. Treat this section as training the "underlying muscle" of rule-spotting and careful counting under time pressure, not as a complete simulation of a visual test. With that boundary clearly stated, two structures repeat throughout this section. A "symbol cycle" is a sequence of symbols that repeats after a fixed number of steps, called its "period" — for example, ▲●■▲●■▲●■... has period 3, because the pattern "▲●■" repeats over and over. To find the symbol at any given position N in a cycle of period p, you do not need to write out the whole sequence: you only need to know where N falls within ONE repeat of the cycle, which you find using the remainder left over after dividing N by p (formally, "(N−1) mod p," counting the very first position as position 1). A "symbol grid" combines this with the row-and-column idea from number grids: instead of a number in each cell, you count how many copies of a symbol appear, and that COUNT follows the same kind of row-step / column-step rule a number grid would.',
          core: 'This section approximates — but does not replace — genuine rotated-figure visual reasoning; treat it as rule-spotting practice, not a full simulation. For a symbol cycle of period p, the symbol at position N is the same as the symbol at position ((N−1) mod p) + 1. For a symbol grid, count the symbols in each cell and treat those counts exactly like a number grid.',
          when: 'Any item showing a repeating run of symbols and asking for a specific later position, or a grid of symbol-groups with one cell to fill in.',
          formulas: ['Cycle of period p: the symbol at position N equals the symbol at position ((N−1) mod p) + 1 within one repeat of the cycle.', 'Symbol grid: convert each cell to a plain count of symbols, then apply the same row-step / column-step logic as a number grid.'],
          intuition: 'A "period" is just a fancy word for "how long before it repeats" — once you know that number, predicting ANY future position becomes pure arithmetic (a remainder calculation) instead of tediously writing out the whole sequence by hand.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'A cycle repeats with period 4: ▲ ● ■ ◆ ▲ ● ■ ◆ ... What symbol is in position 10? Compute (10−1) mod 4 = 9 mod 4 = 1, so position 10 matches the symbol at index 1 within the block (counting the first symbol of the block as index 0): ▲=index0, ●=index1, so the answer is ●. Sanity check by direct counting: positions 1-4 are ▲●■◆, positions 5-8 repeat as ▲●■◆, position 9 starts the block again as ▲, position 10 is ●. Matches.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Two interleaved cycles: odd positions cycle through ★ ♦ (period 2), even positions cycle through ▲ ● ■ (period 3). What is in position 11? Position 11 is odd, so it belongs to the ★♦ cycle. Within odd positions only, position 11 is the 6th odd position (positions 1,3,5,7,9,11), and (6−1) mod 2 = 1, so it is the symbol at index 1 of ★♦, which is ♦.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'A symbol grid shows rows of ★ symbols with counts 1,3,5 in row 1; 2,4,6 in row 2; 3,5,7 in row 3, with the middle cell of row 3 (currently showing 5 stars) hidden. Converting to numbers: this is a row-step-2, column-step-1 grid with base 1 (row r, column c, 0-indexed: 1 + r×1 + c×2... check: row0 = 1,3,5 fits 1+0+0=1, 1+0+2=3, 1+0+4=5 ✓. row1 = 2,4,6 fits 1+1+0=2,1+1+2=4,1+1+4=6 ✓. row2 col1 (the hidden cell) = 1+2+2 = 5, confirming the visible "5" — so if this cell were truly hidden, the same formula would predict 5 stars.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Trying to eyeball a long symbol sequence by physically counting every single symbol from the start instead of using the remainder shortcut — slow and error-prone once positions get large. For interleaved cycles, forgetting to first split into odd/even positions and re-index within just that sub-cycle.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'A cycle repeats with period 3: ● ■ ▲ ● ■ ▲ ... What is in position 7?', options: ['●', '■', '▲', 'Cannot tell'], a: 0, why: '(7−1) mod 3 = 0, matching the first symbol of the block, ●.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'Why does this app say its "abstract reasoning" section is only an approximation?', options: ['Because the questions are too easy', 'Because real visual reasoning tests use rotated/reflected images, which text cannot reproduce', 'Because it only has a few items', 'It is not an approximation, it is identical'], a: 1, why: 'Genuine spatial reasoning tests require mentally rotating real images, a skill text and symbols cannot fully train.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'A cycle repeats with period 5. What is in position 16?', options: ['The same symbol as position 1', 'The same symbol as position 2', 'The same symbol as position 5', 'Cannot be determined'], a: 0, why: '(16−1) mod 5 = 15 mod 5 = 0, matching position 1 of the block.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'In a symbol grid, cell(row,col) = 2 + row×3 + col×1 (0-indexed). How many symbols are at row 2, column 2?', options: ['9', '10', '8', '11'], a: 1, why: '2 + 2×3 + 2×1 = 2+6+2 = 10.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Two interleaved cycles: odd positions cycle A B (period 2), even positions cycle X Y Z (period 3). What symbol is in position 14?', options: ['A', 'B', 'X', 'Y'], a: 2, why: 'Position 14 is even, so it is the 7th even position (2,4,6,8,10,12,14). (7−1) mod 3 = 0, matching the first symbol of the even cycle, X.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Why is "just count every symbol from the start" a poor strategy for finding position 40 of a period-6 cycle under time pressure?', options: ['It is actually the fastest method', 'It wastes time that the remainder shortcut avoids entirely, and invites counting errors', 'Period-6 cycles cannot be solved any other way', 'Position 40 does not exist in a period-6 cycle'], a: 1, why: 'The remainder method gives the answer in one small calculation, no matter how large the position number is.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== R-C =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'R-C', topic: 'Logical Reasoning', title: 'Formal logic: syllogisms, conditionals and constraint puzzles', track: 'reasoning', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'rc1', name: 'Categorical syllogisms: what "valid" actually means, and the undistributed-middle trap',
          primer: 'A "categorical syllogism" is a small logical argument made of exactly two starting statements, called "premises," followed by a "conclusion" — and all three statements are about groups of things, using one of four fixed sentence patterns: "All X are Y," "No X are Y," "Some X are Y," or "Some X are not Y" (where "some" here formally means "at least one," not "a large amount"). Crucially, the two premises between them mention exactly THREE different group-names in total, and one of those three groups — called the "middle term" — appears in BOTH premises but never appears in the conclusion itself; the other two groups each appear in one premise and then again in the conclusion. The single most important thing to understand about the word "valid" in this context is that it has NOTHING to do with whether the conclusion sounds true or reasonable in the real world — a syllogism is "valid" if and only if the conclusion is FORCED to be true in absolutely every possible situation where both premises happen to be true, with no exceptions whatsoever; if you can imagine even one single made-up scenario where both premises hold but the conclusion fails, the syllogism is "invalid," no matter how sensible the conclusion sounds. This is why the fastest and most reliable way to test a syllogism is not to ask "does this sound right?" but to actively hunt for a counterexample: try to picture three overlapping groups (or literally sketch three overlapping circles) arranged so that both premises are satisfied, and check whether the conclusion is then forced or merely possible. By far the most common invalid pattern that LOOKS convincing is called the "undistributed middle term": this happens when the middle term is only ever described as containing SOME or ALL of each of the other two groups SEPARATELY (for instance, "All P are M" and "All S are M"), without either premise ever describing how those two groups relate to EACH OTHER inside M — since M can easily be large enough to contain P and S as two completely separate, non-overlapping parts, no relationship between S and P is actually guaranteed.',
          core: 'A syllogism is valid only if the conclusion is forced to be true in EVERY situation consistent with both premises — not just in the situation that sounds most natural. Actively search for a counterexample (a scenario satisfying both premises but breaking the conclusion) rather than judging by "does it sound true."',
          when: 'Any "All/Some/No ... therefore ..." argument you must classify as Valid or Invalid.',
          formulas: ['A syllogism has exactly 3 group-names: the middle term M (in both premises, never in the conclusion), plus S and P (each in one premise and in the conclusion).', 'Undistributed-middle test: if BOTH premises only place S and P separately "inside" M (e.g. "All S are M" and "All P are M"), with no premise relating S and P to EACH OTHER through M, the syllogism is invalid.', 'Valid forms are those where at least one premise pins down enough of M\'s relationship to S or P that no counterexample can be built.'],
          intuition: 'Think of the middle term as a big room, and S and P as two smaller groups of people who are each SOMEWHERE inside that room. Just because both groups are in the same room does not mean they are standing anywhere near each other — a valid syllogism needs a premise that actually forces some genuine overlap or containment between S and P, not just a shared room.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: '"All dogs are mammals. All poodles are dogs. Therefore, all poodles are mammals." Middle term: dogs (appears in both premises, not in the conclusion). Try to build a counterexample: could there be a poodle that is a dog but not a mammal? No — since poodles are entirely inside dogs, and dogs are entirely inside mammals, poodles must be entirely inside mammals too. No counterexample exists. Valid.' },
            { level: 2, text: '"All cats are mammals. All dogs are mammals. Therefore, all dogs are cats." Middle term: mammals. Try to build a counterexample: imagine mammals as a big room containing two separate, non-overlapping corners — one corner is "cats," the other corner is "dogs." Both premises are satisfied (cats are in the room, dogs are in the room), but dogs are clearly NOT inside the cats corner. This counterexample breaks the conclusion. Invalid — this is the undistributed-middle trap: both premises only place the two groups inside the same room without relating them to each other.' },
            { level: 3, text: '"No reptiles are warm-blooded. Some pets are reptiles. Therefore, some pets are not warm-blooded." Middle term: reptiles. The pets that are reptiles (guaranteed to exist by the second premise) are, by the first premise, guaranteed NOT to be warm-blooded — so those specific pets are pets that are not warm-blooded. Try to build a counterexample: is there any way for a pet-that-is-a-reptile to still be warm-blooded? No, the first premise rules that out entirely. No counterexample exists. Valid.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Judging validity by whether the conclusion sounds true in real life. "All roses are flowers, some flowers fade quickly, therefore some roses fade quickly" SOUNDS plausible, but is actually invalid — the "flowers that fade quickly" from the second premise are never guaranteed to be roses specifically, they could be an entirely different, non-overlapping set of flowers.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'A syllogism is "valid" when:', options: ['The conclusion sounds true in real life', 'The conclusion is forced to be true in every scenario where both premises are true', 'Most people would agree with it', 'It uses the word "all" in both premises'], a: 1, why: 'Validity is about the conclusion being logically forced, not about real-world plausibility.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'In a syllogism, the "middle term" is the group that:', options: ['Appears in the conclusion only', 'Appears in both premises but never in the conclusion', 'Appears in exactly one premise and the conclusion', 'Is always the biggest group'], a: 1, why: 'By definition, the middle term links the two premises together and is then eliminated from the conclusion.' },
            { level: 2, q: '"All bankers wear suits. All lawyers wear suits. Therefore, all lawyers are bankers." Valid or invalid?', options: ['Valid', 'Invalid'], a: 1, why: 'Both premises only place bankers and lawyers separately inside "people who wear suits," with no premise relating them to each other — undistributed middle. Invalid.' },
            { level: 2, q: '"All employees at Desk X passed the Series 7 exam. Maria passed the Series 7 exam. Therefore, Maria works at Desk X." Valid or invalid?', options: ['Valid', 'Invalid'], a: 1, why: 'The premise only says Desk X employees passed the exam, not that everyone who passed the exam works at Desk X — undistributed middle. Invalid.' },
            { level: 3, q: '"No fish are mammals. All salmon are fish. Therefore, no salmon are mammals." Valid or invalid?', options: ['Valid', 'Invalid'], a: 0, why: 'Since salmon are entirely inside fish, and fish and mammals never overlap at all, salmon and mammals cannot overlap either. No counterexample is possible. Valid.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'What is the fastest way to test a syllogism\'s validity under time pressure?', options: ['Ask whether the conclusion sounds reasonable', 'Actively try to construct a scenario where both premises hold but the conclusion fails', 'Count how many premises use the word "all"', 'Assume it is valid unless it uses "no" twice'], a: 1, why: 'Actively hunting for a counterexample is the direct test of the actual definition of validity.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'rc2', name: 'Conditional reasoning: modus ponens and modus tollens versus the two classic fallacies',
          primer: 'A "conditional statement" is any sentence of the form "If [something happens], then [something else happens]," which logicians shorten to "if P then Q," where P is called the "antecedent" (the condition that comes first) and Q is called the "consequent" (the result that is claimed to follow). The single most important thing to internalize about a conditional is that it is a ONE-DIRECTIONAL promise: "if P then Q" guarantees that Q will happen WHENEVER P happens, but it makes absolutely no promise about what happens when P does NOT happen (Q might still happen anyway, for a completely different reason), and it makes no claim that P is the ONLY possible way to make Q happen. Starting from a conditional "if P then Q" plus one additional fact, there are exactly four combinations logicians have named, and only two of them are valid. "Modus ponens" (Latin for "the way that affirms") says: given "if P then Q" and given that P actually happened, you may validly conclude Q happened — this is the direct, forward use of the conditional exactly as promised. "Modus tollens" (Latin for "the way that denies") says: given "if P then Q" and given that Q did NOT happen, you may validly conclude P did not happen either — this works because if P HAD happened, Q would have been forced to happen too (by the conditional itself), so Q\'s absence rules P out; this is a valid BACKWARD use of the conditional. The two fallacies (invalid patterns that sound deceptively similar) are: "affirming the consequent" — given "if P then Q" and given that Q happened, wrongly concluding P happened, which ignores that Q could have been caused by something else entirely; and "denying the antecedent" — given "if P then Q" and given that P did NOT happen, wrongly concluding Q did not happen either, which ignores that the conditional never claimed P was Q\'s only possible cause.',
          core: 'From "if P then Q": knowing P happened lets you validly conclude Q happened (modus ponens). Knowing Q did NOT happen lets you validly conclude P did not happen (modus tollens). Knowing Q happened does NOT let you conclude P happened (affirming the consequent — a fallacy). Knowing P did NOT happen does NOT let you conclude Q did not happen (denying the antecedent — a fallacy).',
          when: 'Any "if X then Y... [some further fact]... therefore..." argument you must classify as valid or invalid.',
          formulas: ['Modus ponens (valid): if P then Q; P is true; therefore Q is true.', 'Modus tollens (valid): if P then Q; Q is false; therefore P is false.', 'Affirming the consequent (invalid): if P then Q; Q is true; therefore P is true. — does NOT follow.', 'Denying the antecedent (invalid): if P then Q; P is false; therefore Q is false. — does NOT follow.'],
          intuition: 'Picture "if P then Q" as a one-way street sign: traffic is guaranteed to flow from P towards Q, but nothing stops traffic reaching Q by an entirely different road, and nothing is promised about traffic that never entered from P in the first place.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: '"If a bond is downgraded, its yield rises. This bond was downgraded." Does its yield rise? Yes — this is modus ponens: the antecedent (downgraded) is confirmed true, so the consequent (yield rises) is forced to follow exactly as promised. Valid.' },
            { level: 2, text: '"If the server load exceeds 90%, a new instance is launched. No new instance was launched." Did the server load exceed 90%? No, it did not — this is modus tollens: the consequent (new instance launched) is confirmed FALSE, so the antecedent (load exceeded 90%) must also be false, since if it had been true a new instance would have been forced to launch. Valid.' },
            { level: 3, text: '"If it rains overnight, the pitch is closed in the morning. The pitch is closed this morning." Can you conclude it rained overnight? No — this commits affirming the consequent: the pitch being closed (the consequent) is confirmed, but the conditional never said rain was the ONLY possible reason for the pitch to be closed (maintenance, frost, or an event could also close it). The correct answer is "cannot say," not "yes."' }
          ],
          trap: 'Silently assuming "if P then Q" also means "if Q then P" (that is affirming the consequent) or "if not P then not Q" (that is denying the antecedent) — a conditional only ever licenses reasoning in the two directions covered by modus ponens and modus tollens, never the other two.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: '"If P then Q" and "P is true." What follows?', options: ['Q is true', 'Q is false', 'Nothing follows', 'P is false'], a: 0, why: 'This is modus ponens — directly applying the conditional forward.' },
            { level: 1, q: '"If P then Q" and "Q is false." What follows?', options: ['P is true', 'P is false', 'Nothing follows', 'Q is true'], a: 1, why: 'This is modus tollens — if P had been true, Q would have been forced true too, so Q\'s falsity rules P out.' },
            { level: 2, q: '"If P then Q" and "Q is true." Can you validly conclude P is true?', options: ['Yes, always', 'No — this is affirming the consequent, a fallacy', 'Only if P and Q are the same statement', 'Only if the conditional is reversible'], a: 1, why: 'Q being true does not rule out other causes of Q — the conditional never claimed P was the only way to get Q.' },
            { level: 2, q: '"If P then Q" and "P is false." Can you validly conclude Q is false?', options: ['Yes, always', 'No — this is denying the antecedent, a fallacy', 'Only if Q never happens otherwise', 'Only if P and Q are opposites'], a: 1, why: 'The conditional says nothing about what happens when P does not occur — Q could still happen for another reason.' },
            { level: 3, q: '"If a trade breaches the risk limit, it is automatically closed. This trade was not automatically closed." What follows?', options: ['The trade breached the risk limit', 'The trade did not breach the risk limit', 'Nothing follows', 'The trade limit does not exist'], a: 1, why: 'Modus tollens: the consequent (automatically closed) is false, so the antecedent (breached the limit) must be false too.' },
            { level: 3, q: '"If a trade breaches the risk limit, it is automatically closed. This trade WAS automatically closed." What follows?', options: ['The trade breached the risk limit', 'Cannot say — closing could have another cause', 'The trade did not breach the risk limit', 'The risk limit was raised'], a: 1, why: 'This is affirming the consequent — the trade being closed does not prove the risk limit was the cause, since other reasons for closing a trade may exist.' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'rc3', name: 'Constraint puzzles: chaining clues until only one arrangement survives',
          primer: 'A "constraint puzzle" (sometimes called an ordering or logic-grid puzzle) gives you a fixed group of people, items or events, and a list of "clues" — short factual statements that each rule OUT some of the possible arrangements without necessarily pinning down the full picture by themselves — and asks you to determine one specific detail (such as "who is in position 3?") that is uniquely determined once ALL the clues are combined together. The two most common clue types are "absolute" clues, which state a fact directly (e.g. "Maria is in position 2," or "the meeting with Client A happens before the meeting with Client B") and "relative" clues, which only describe a relationship between two items without saying exactly where either one is (e.g. "Tom is immediately to the left of Priya," which fixes their relationship to each other but not their absolute position in the full lineup). The key insight that makes these puzzles solvable quickly is that NO SINGLE clue is expected to solve the whole puzzle by itself — clues are meant to be combined, narrowing down the space of possible arrangements step by step, the same way solving a Sudoku cell never comes from staring at one rule in isolation but from combining several constraints that intersect at that cell. A reliable, fast strategy is to start with whichever clue gives you the MOST certain, most absolute piece of information (ideally a clue that directly fixes one item\'s exact position), place that item, and then work outward: for every remaining clue, ask "does this now force a NEW item into a specific position, given what I have already placed?" and keep repeating that question, placing one new item at a time, until every position is filled. If you ever find yourself stuck with two or more arrangements that both seem to satisfy everything you have placed so far, that means you have not yet used a clue you skipped over, or you have misapplied a relative clue by treating it as if it were more specific than it actually is.',
          core: 'Start from the most absolute (position-fixing) clue you have, place that one item, then repeatedly ask "does any remaining clue now force a new item into place, given what I\'ve already fixed?" — chaining outward one certain placement at a time, rather than trying to solve the whole puzzle from a single clue.',
          when: 'Any puzzle giving several people/items and a set of clues, asking you to determine one specific position or arrangement detail.',
          formulas: ['Absolute clue: fixes one item\'s exact position directly ("X is in position k").', 'Relative clue: fixes a relationship between two items without fixing either one\'s absolute position ("X is immediately left of Y," "X is somewhere left of Y").', 'Solving order: place from absolute clues first, then repeatedly re-scan relative clues to see which ones now become absolute given what is already placed.'],
          intuition: 'Think of each relative clue as a small, movable jigsaw piece — two items glued together in some relationship, but the whole glued piece can still slide freely until an absolute clue (or another glued piece) pins it down to one specific spot in the final row.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'Four people A, B, C, D sit in a row of 4 seats. Clues: "B is in seat 2." "A is immediately to the left of C." Start with the absolute clue: B is in seat 2. The relative clue (A immediately left of C) must occupy two adjacent seats among the remaining {1, 3, 4} — the only adjacent pair available is {3, 4}, so A is in seat 3 and C is in seat 4. That leaves D in seat 1.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Four runners finish a race with no ties. Clues: "X finishes before Y." "Z finishes after Y but before W." Chain the two relative clues together: X < Y (from clue 1), and Y < Z < W (from clue 2). Combining them: X < Y < Z < W. Even though neither clue alone fixes anyone\'s finishing position, chaining them together fixes the entire order: X first, Y second, Z third, W fourth.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Five people sit in seats 1-5. Clues: "E is in seat 5." "C is somewhere to the left of D." "B is immediately to the right of A." "A is not in seat 1." Start with the absolute clue: E is in seat 5, leaving seats 1-4 for A, B, C, D. Clue 4 rules out A in seat 1. Clue 3 (B immediately right of A) needs two adjacent seats among {1,2,3,4}; since A cannot be in seat 1, the only remaining adjacent options are A=2,B=3 or A=3,B=4. If A=3,B=4, only seats 1,2 remain for C,D, but clue 2 needs C left of D, so C=1,D=2 — check this is consistent, giving order C,D,A,B,E. If A=2,B=3, seats 1,4 remain for C,D with C left of D forcing C=1,D=4 — also consistent, giving C,A,B,D,E. Two different arrangements both satisfy every clue, so this puzzle (as stated) does not have a unique solution — a well-formed puzzle would include one more clue to break this tie.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Treating a "somewhere to the left of" clue as if it meant "immediately to the left of" — the first only fixes relative ORDER, not adjacency, and combining it incorrectly with other clues can smuggle in a false certainty that is not actually there.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'An "absolute" clue is one that:', options: ['Describes a relationship between two items without fixing either\'s position', 'Fixes one specific item\'s exact position directly', 'Is always the first clue given', 'Can be ignored if there are enough other clues'], a: 1, why: 'By definition, an absolute clue names a specific, fixed position.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'What is the recommended first step in solving a constraint puzzle?', options: ['Guess an arrangement and check it against all clues', 'Start from the most absolute (position-fixing) clue and place that item first', 'Start from the longest clue', 'Ignore relative clues entirely'], a: 1, why: 'Placing a certain, absolute fact first gives you a stable anchor to chain the remaining clues from.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Clues: "P is immediately left of Q." "Q is immediately left of R." What can you conclude?', options: ['Nothing, since neither clue fixes an absolute position', 'P, Q, R must appear consecutively in that exact order somewhere in the row', 'P is definitely in seat 1', 'R is definitely in the last seat'], a: 1, why: 'Chaining the two relative clues fixes their RELATIVE block (P then Q then R, consecutively) even without yet knowing the absolute seat numbers.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Four people sit in seats 1-4. Clue: "M is in seat 1." Clue: "N is immediately to the right of M." Where is N?', options: ['Seat 2', 'Seat 3', 'Seat 4', 'Cannot be determined'], a: 0, why: 'M is fixed at seat 1 (absolute clue); "immediately to the right" then forces N into seat 2.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'A puzzle gives clues that result in two different arrangements both satisfying every clue. What does this tell you?', options: ['The puzzle is working as intended', 'The puzzle (as given) does not have a unique solution — a clue is missing', 'You must have made an arithmetic error', 'Constraint puzzles always have multiple valid answers'], a: 1, why: 'A well-formed constraint puzzle is specifically constructed so that its clues, combined, pin down exactly one arrangement — finding two survivors means the given clue set is incomplete.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Clue: "C is somewhere to the left of D." Is this the same as "C is immediately to the left of D"?', options: ['Yes, they mean exactly the same thing', 'No — "somewhere to the left" only fixes relative order, allowing any number of seats between them', 'No — "somewhere to the left" actually means to the right', 'They are only the same if there are exactly 2 seats'], a: 1, why: '"Somewhere to the left" is a weaker, order-only clue; "immediately to the left" additionally requires adjacency. Treating them as identical smuggles in false certainty.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    /* =============================== R-D =============================== */
    {
      unit: 'R-D', topic: 'Verbal Reasoning', title: 'Verbal reasoning: analogies, classification and statement logic', track: 'reasoning', concepts: [
        {
          id: 'rd1', name: 'Verbal analogies: naming the exact relation before you look at the options',
          primer: 'A verbal analogy question presents one pair of words that are related to each other in some specific way, and asks you to complete a second pair with the SAME kind of relationship — usually written as "WORD1 is to WORD2 as WORD3 is to ?" The single biggest speed-and-accuracy improvement available on this question type is a simple discipline: name the relationship between the first pair, out loud or in your head, in a short precise sentence, BEFORE you even glance at the four answer options. If you skip this step and jump straight to the options, your brain will naturally gravitate toward whichever option merely "feels associated" with the third word in some vague way, which is exactly how the wrong-but-tempting distractor options are designed to catch you. Common relationship types include: synonym (the two words mean the same thing, like HAPPY:JOYFUL), antonym (the two words mean opposite things, like HOT:COLD), part-to-whole (the first word names a piece, and the second word names the complete thing it belongs to, like WHEEL:CAR), cause-and-effect (the first word directly produces the second as a result, like SPARK:FIRE), tool-to-user (the first word is an instrument, and the second word is the specific professional who characteristically wields it, like SCALPEL:SURGEON), and category membership (the first word is one specific example, and the second word is the broader class it belongs to, like ROSE:FLOWER). Once you have named the relationship precisely, apply that EXACT same sentence template to each of the four options in turn, substituting the third word and testing each candidate: "does [third word] relate to [option] in that same specific way?" — options that are merely topically related to the third word (same general subject area, same rough "vibe") but fail the precise relationship test must be eliminated, even if they seem tempting.',
          core: 'Before looking at the options, state the relationship between the first pair as a precise sentence ("A means the same as B," "A is a part of B," "A causes B," ...). Then test each option by substituting it into that exact same sentence with the third word — eliminate anything that is merely topically associated but fails the precise test.',
          when: 'Any "WORD1 is to WORD2 as WORD3 is to ?" analogy question.',
          formulas: ['Step 1: state the stem relationship as an explicit sentence using WORD1 and WORD2.', 'Step 2: for each option, substitute WORD3 and the option into the SAME sentence template and check whether it still reads true.', 'A correct option must satisfy the exact relationship type, not just share a topic or "feel" with WORD3.'],
          intuition: 'The wrong options in a well-made analogy question are specifically chosen because they share SOME connection to the third word — just the wrong KIND of connection. Naming the relationship first turns a vague "which one feels right?" judgment into a mechanical, checkable test.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'BEGIN is to START as CONCLUDE is to ? Step 1: name the relationship — "BEGIN means the same thing as START" (synonym). Step 2: test each option with "CONCLUDE means the same thing as ___": FINISH (yes, true) beats CONTINUE (means something different — to keep going, not to end), PAUSE (means to temporarily stop, not conclude) and OPEN (roughly opposite in meaning). FINISH is correct.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'BRANCH is to TREE as ROOM is to ? Step 1: name the relationship — "a BRANCH is a part of a TREE" (part-to-whole). Step 2: test each option with "a ROOM is a part of ___": HOUSE (yes, true and at the same level of containment) beats FURNITURE (furniture is inside a room, the wrong direction), DOOR (also a part, like room — not the whole) and NEIGHBORHOOD (technically true but one level too broad, breaking the exact level of the original pair). HOUSE is correct.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'GOLD is to METAL as OXYGEN is to ? Step 1: name the relationship precisely — "GOLD is classified by its MATERIAL TYPE as a METAL" (not simply "GOLD is a chemical element," which would be true but is a different, broader classification). Step 2: test each option with "OXYGEN is classified by its material type as ___": GAS (yes, this is the parallel material-type classification) beats ELEMENT (true of gold too, so it fails to mirror the SPECIFIC relation used in the stem — it does not distinguish the intended pattern), LIQUID (factually wrong) and COMPOUND (also factually wrong, oxygen gas is an element). GAS is correct — this example shows why naming the relationship PRECISELY (not just "some kind of category") matters.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Picking the option that is most strongly associated with the third word in everyday conversation, rather than the option that satisfies the SAME precise relationship as the stem pair — for example picking a word\'s near-synonym when the actual relationship intended was "effect of," or picking a broader category that happens to also be technically true of both words in the stem, obscuring which specific relationship was intended.',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'What should you do BEFORE looking at the four options in an analogy question?', options: ['Skim all four options quickly for the "best feel"', 'Name the exact relationship between the first pair as a precise sentence', 'Pick the option that appeared first', 'Eliminate the longest word'], a: 1, why: 'Naming the relationship first turns the question into a checkable test instead of a vague impression.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'HOT is to COLD as FAST is to ?', options: ['SLOW', 'QUICK', 'WARM', 'RAPID'], a: 0, why: 'HOT:COLD is an antonym pair; SLOW is the antonym of FAST. QUICK and RAPID are synonyms of FAST, the wrong relationship.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'SCALPEL is to SURGEON as GAVEL is to ?', options: ['LAWYER', 'JUDGE', 'COURT', 'JURY'], a: 1, why: 'The relationship is tool-to-specific-user; a JUDGE is the one who wields a gavel, not a lawyer, court (a place) or jury.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Why is a "topically related" option often a trap in analogy questions?', options: ['Because topically related options are always correct', 'Because it shares SOME connection to the third word, but not the SAME precise relationship as the stem pair', 'Because analogy questions never have topically related options', 'Because it is always the longest option'], a: 1, why: 'Distractor options are deliberately chosen to feel plausible through a loose topical connection while failing the exact relationship test.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'OAK is to TREE as COBRA is to ?', options: ['SNAKE', 'REPTILE', 'LIZARD', 'AMPHIBIAN'], a: 0, why: 'OAK:TREE is one specific level of category (a type of tree, not "plant" in general). SNAKE matches that same level for COBRA; REPTILE is a level too broad, even though technically true.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'DROUGHT is to FAMINE as NEGLIGENCE is to ?', options: ['CARELESSNESS', 'ACCIDENT', 'SAFETY', 'INSURANCE'], a: 1, why: 'The relationship is cause-and-effect: DROUGHT causes FAMINE, and NEGLIGENCE causes an ACCIDENT. CARELESSNESS is merely a synonym of negligence, not its effect — a classic trap of confusing a synonym with the actual answer to "what does this cause?"' }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: 'rd2', name: 'Classification, and statement reasoning: True, False, or Cannot Say',
          primer: 'A "classification" (or "odd one out") question gives you five or six words and tells you that all but one share a specific property, asking you to identify the one that breaks it — the strategy here mirrors numeric odd-one-out exactly: state the shared category explicitly in words first ("these are all X"), then test the remaining candidate against that exact category, rather than eliminating options purely by a vague sense of "these seem to go together." A "statement reasoning" question (used in well-known verbal-reasoning assessments) gives you a short passage of information that you must accept as completely true for the purposes of the question, followed by a proposed conclusion, and asks you to classify that conclusion into one of three categories: "True" means the conclusion is DIRECTLY and NECESSARILY forced by the passage — no extra outside knowledge or assumption is needed, just careful reading of exactly what was stated. "False" means the conclusion directly CONTRADICTS something the passage establishes as fact — again, no outside knowledge needed, just a direct clash with what was given. "Cannot Say" (sometimes called "cannot determine") is the category most people misuse under time pressure: it applies whenever the passage simply does NOT give enough information to decide the conclusion either way — the conclusion might well be true in reality, and might even sound very plausible or likely, but the passage itself does not establish it as a logical certainty, and it does not directly contradict the passage either. The single most important discipline for this question type is refusing to bring in outside real-world knowledge, plausibility, or common sense that was not actually stated in the passage — your only job is to determine what the GIVEN SENTENCES, and only those sentences, logically force or rule out; a conclusion that "seems very likely to be true" based on how the real world usually works is still "Cannot Say" if the passage itself never actually establishes it.',
          core: 'Classification: state the shared category as a sentence, then test the outlier against it. Statement reasoning: "True" = the passage forces the conclusion; "False" = the passage directly contradicts the conclusion; "Cannot Say" = the passage simply does not give enough information either way, REGARDLESS of how plausible the conclusion sounds in the real world.',
          when: 'Odd-one-out word lists, and any passage-plus-conclusion question asking you to judge True / False / Cannot Say (or similar three-way logical categories).',
          formulas: ['Classification: name the shared property in words, then check the candidate word against that exact wording.', 'Statement reasoning: True ⇔ passage logically forces the conclusion. False ⇔ passage logically contradicts the conclusion. Cannot Say ⇔ neither — insufficient information, no matter how plausible the conclusion sounds.'],
          intuition: 'A "Cannot Say" verdict is not an admission of confusion — it is often the MOST rigorous, correct answer, precisely because it resists the very natural temptation to quietly fill in a gap in the passage with real-world assumptions that were never actually stated.',
          examples: [
            { level: 1, text: 'Which does not belong: Piano, Violin, Guitar, Harp, Flute? Name the shared property: "Piano, Violin, Guitar and Harp all produce sound from vibrating STRINGS." Test Flute against that exact property: a flute produces sound from a column of air, not strings — it fails the stated property. Flute is the odd one out.' },
            { level: 2, text: 'Passage: "Most senior partners at the firm studied economics or finance as undergraduates. Raj is a senior partner at the firm." Conclusion: "Raj studied economics or finance as an undergraduate." The passage says MOST, not ALL, senior partners fit this description — so Raj could plausibly be among the minority who did not, and the passage gives no way to rule that out. Even though it sounds likely that Raj fits the majority pattern, the passage does not force it. Cannot Say.' },
            { level: 3, text: 'Passage: "If the quarterly revenue target is missed, the bonus pool is reduced by 20%. The bonus pool was not reduced this quarter." Conclusion: "The quarterly revenue target was met this quarter." This is a direct application of modus tollens: the passage guarantees a reduction whenever the target is missed; since no reduction happened, the target cannot have been missed, so it must have been met. This is fully forced by the passage, with no outside assumption needed. True.' }
          ],
          trap: 'Marking a conclusion "True" because it sounds very plausible or is probably true in the real world, when the passage itself never actually establishes it as a certainty — this is the single most common statement-reasoning error, and it specifically means confusing "likely" with "logically forced." The opposite trap also occurs: marking something "Cannot Say" when the passage actually does contain a valid modus ponens/modus tollens chain that forces a definite True or False answer — always check for a hidden valid chain before defaulting to "Cannot Say."',
          checks: [
            { level: 1, q: 'In a classification question, what should you do before checking the candidate word?', options: ['State the shared property in words explicitly', 'Guess based on which word looks longest', 'Assume the first word listed is always correct', 'Skip straight to the answer that feels different'], a: 0, why: 'Naming the shared property precisely turns the question into a checkable test rather than a vague impression.' },
            { level: 1, q: 'In statement reasoning, "Cannot Say" means:', options: ['The passage is poorly written', 'The passage does not give enough information to decide True or False, regardless of real-world plausibility', 'The conclusion is definitely false', 'You should guess randomly'], a: 1, why: '"Cannot Say" specifically means insufficient information in the passage, not "confusing" or "poorly written."' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Which does not belong: Copper, Aluminum, Plastic, Iron, Silver?', options: ['Copper', 'Plastic', 'Iron', 'Silver'], a: 1, why: 'Copper, Aluminum, Iron and Silver are all metals; Plastic is a synthetic non-metal material.' },
            { level: 2, q: 'Passage: "Since the new compliance software was installed in March, flagged transactions have risen every month." Conclusion: "The software caused the increase." True, False, or Cannot Say?', options: ['True', 'False', 'Cannot Say'], a: 2, why: 'Matching timing alone does not establish causation — an unstated factor could explain the rise. Cannot Say.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Passage: "Every trader who exceeded their risk limit received a formal warning. Daniel received a formal warning." Conclusion: "Daniel exceeded his risk limit." True, False, or Cannot Say?', options: ['True', 'False', 'Cannot Say'], a: 2, why: 'This is affirming the consequent — the rule never said warnings are given ONLY for exceeding the limit, so a warning does not prove the cause. Cannot Say.' },
            { level: 3, q: 'Passage: "No employee may access the client database without two-factor authentication, per policy. Every access attempt yesterday used two-factor authentication successfully." Conclusion: "At least one access attempt yesterday violated the policy." True, False, or Cannot Say?', options: ['True', 'False', 'Cannot Say'], a: 1, why: 'The passage states directly that EVERY attempt used two-factor authentication, which is a direct contradiction of the claim that at least one violated the policy. False.' }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ];

  global.QTL_LESSONS_REASONING = L;
})(window);
